# block_device_server manifest — the policy for the task that drives the disks
# (#511).
#
# 🔴 IT HAD NONE, AND THAT IS WHY IT NEEDED ONE.  libcap prefers the per-task
# cap port bootstrap stamps into TASK_CAP_PORT and falls back to the
# well-known cap_server when no per-task port was provisioned -- and bootstrap
# provisions one only for a task that ships a manifest.  So a server with no
# policy file did not get a permissive answer from the manifest check: it
# never reached the check at all.
#
# 🔑 The census that found this counted three requests arriving on the
# well-known port in one boot, two of them RESOURCE_PCI_DEVICE -- this task
# claiming its two controllers, with nothing consulted about whether it may.
# The one task on the machine that most needed a policy was the one with no
# way to be subject to one.
#
# ⚠️ Two classes and not two addresses, for the reason dma_reclaim_test's
# manifest gives: this tree's AHCI is 00:04.0 under one QEMU command line and
# 00:1f.2 under another, and a manifest is written before the machine exists.
# The kernel reads the class out of the device's own configuration space and
# requires it to agree with what was declared, so naming a class is a claim
# about the KIND of hardware this server drives, checked against the hardware.

name block_device_server

# RESOURCE_PCI_DEVICE (5), class 0x010601 -- SATA AHCI -- DMA|MMIO|IRQ (0x7).
#
# The ich9-ahci controller and its two disks.  block_server.c asks for exactly
# this with `cap_request(RESOURCE_PCI_DEVICE, class_rev >> 8, ...)', so the
# number here is the one the hardware reports and not a name chosen twice.
required 5 0x010601 0x7

# RESOURCE_PCI_DEVICE (5), class 0x010000 -- SCSI mass storage -- DMA|MMIO|IRQ.
#
# ⚠️ This is virtio-blk, which reports itself as SCSI rather than as anything
# virtio-shaped.  It is the BOOT disk: refusing it here would take the whole
# boot with it, which is the loudest possible way to find out that a class
# number was wrong.
required 5 0x010000 0x7

# RESOURCE_DMA_BUFFER (10), any buffer, device READ|WRITE (0x3).
#
# 🔑 `any' is the only honest thing this line can say, and that is a fact
# about the resource rather than a looseness: a manifest is written before the
# system runs and a DMA buffer exists for four milliseconds.  This says
# whether the task may hold buffer capabilities at all; the KERNEL says
# whether a particular buffer is this task's to give away, because it made the
# allocation.
required 10 any 0x3
